                <head>No. 408.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Curry.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1886-10-22T17:15:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi>
                            22, 1886—5.15 p.m</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Bayard informs Mr. Curry that the language of section 4228 is regarded by the
                    President as exactly prescribing his course, and that cargoes from any foreign
                    country are included thereunder; that he can assure the Government of Spain that
                    the commerce to which these words refer is small in amount, and that the
                    advantages which the shipping act of June 26, 1884, accords to the commerce of
                    Spain are of great value; that no more is requested by the United States than is
                    granted to Spain; that it is the desire of the American Government to prevent
                    alternative discriminations, which are productive of injury to both
                    countries.</p>
            </div>
